; A division that actually divides by zero, and the only fixture here
; whose two sides must agree on an EXIT STATUS rather than on stdout
; alone.
;
; WHY THIS FIXTURE EXISTS. `codegen.ax` wraps `/` and `%` in a zero
; test, a trap block calling `@__axiom_div_by_zero` and a
; continuation. Until the IR carried that guard it represented a
; division as ONE instruction, so an emitter written from the IR would
; have dropped the trap - and, worse, the differential could not see
; it: `mireval.ax`'s `sdiv` arm was Axiom's own `/`, which is guarded
; when the evaluator itself is compiled, so the evaluator trapped
; because its HOST trapped. Measured before the fix: native and
; evaluated output were byte-identical and both exited 72 with the IR
; saying nothing whatever about traps.
;
; So two things had to change together, and this fixture is what holds
; them together. `mireval.ax`'s arithmetic is TOTAL now - `sdiv` and
; `srem` answer 0 on a zero divisor instead of trapping - and the trap
; comes from an `MT_TRAP` terminator the lowering planted. Ablate
; either and this case goes red: drop the guard from the lowering and
; the evaluator runs on to print 20 lines and exit 0 against the
; native's 5 and 72; un-total the arithmetic and the evaluator is
; trapping for the host's reason again, which the ablation in §8
; catches by leaving the IR well formed and the answer wrong.
;
; `probe(k)` is `100 / (5 - k)`, so k = 5 divides by zero on the sixth
; line. Five lines reach stdout before it, which is why this fixture
; carries a `.lines` count of 5 rather than the bank's usual 20.
(import IO)

(:: probe (-> Int Int))

(fn (probe n) (/ 100 (- 5 n)))

(:: emit (-> Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (emit k)
  (if (>= k 20)
    0
    {
      (let ((v (probe k)))
        (println "{v}")
      )
      (emit (+ k 1))
    }
  )
)

(:: main Int)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (main) (emit 0))
